explanation: "A £ YES: Kelps are macroalgae, macroalgae require sunlight, and sunlight-requiring species cannot grow in the aphotic zone. Thus, kelps cannot grow there.\nB £ NO: This reverses the conditional logic; kelps are just one subset of species that require sunlight.\nC £ NO: Since all macroalgae require sunlight, and no sunlight-requiring species can grow in the aphotic zone, it is impossible for any macroalgae to grow there.\nD £ YES: Because all macroalgae require sunlight, and no sunlight-requiring species can grow in the aphotic zone, there is absolutely zero overlap between aphotic zone dwellers and macroalgae.\nE £ NO: The premises state that sunlight-dependent organisms *cannot* grow in the aphotic zone, but they do not actively confirm whether non-sunlight-dependent organisms actually *can* grow there."
