explanation: "A £ YES: Most old-growth forests harbor beetles and are banned from logging. Since all old-growth forests contain trees over 200 years old, those beetle-harboring old-growth forests represent a group of over-200-year-old tree areas banned from logging.\nB £ NO: Only *most* old-growth forests harbor the beetles that trigger the logging ban; the remaining old-growth forests might not harbor them and might be open to logging.\nC £ YES: This is the contrapositive of the third premise. If beetle habitats cannot be logged, then logged habitats cannot contain beetles.\nD £ NO: We know most *old-growth* forests have beetles, but old-growth forests might only be a small fraction of all forests with 200+ year old trees, so we cannot apply 'most' to the broader group.\nE £ NO: The premises don't tell us about other reasons for logging bans, so we cannot confirm if there are non-tree-related habitats that are banned from logging."
